'''2.''' Heated; having the sensation of heat; exhibiting the effects of being heated to a moderate degree; hence, flushed.
{{smaller block|<poem>’Twas well, indeed, when ''warm'' with wine,
To pledge them with a kindly tear.</poem>
{{right|''Tennyson'', In Memoriam, xc.}}}}
'''3.''' Communicating a sensation of warmth, or a moderate degree of heat: as, a ''warm'' fire; ''warm'' weather.—'''4.''' Subject to or characterized by the prevalence of a comparatively high temperature, or of moderate heat: as, a ''warm'' climate; ''warm'' countries.—'''5.''' Intimate; close; fast: as, ''warm'' friends.—'''6.''' Hearty; earnest: as, a ''warm'' welcome; ''warm'' thanks.
{{smaller block|The conduct of Hampden in the affair of the ship-money met with the ''warm'' approbation of every respectable Royalist in England.
{{right|''Macaulay'', Hallam’s Const. Hist.}}}}
'''7.''' Fresh: said of a scent or trail.—'''8.''' Close to something that is sought, as in games involving search or guessing; on the right track; on the way to success, as in searching or hunting for something. [Colloq.]
{{smaller block|He ’s ''warm''—he ’s getting cold—he ’s getting colder and colder—he ’s freezing.
{{right|''Dickens'', Our Mutual Friend, iii. 6.}}}}
'''9.''' Comfortable; well-off; moderately rich; in easy circumstances. [Colloq.]
{{smaller block|''Water-Camlet.'' Believe it, I am a poor commoner.<br>''Sir F. Cres.'' Come, you are ''warm'', and blest with a fair wife.
{{right|''Middleton'', Anything for a Quiet Life, i. 1.}}}}
{{smaller block|We have been thinking of marrying her to one of your tenants, . . . a ''warm'' man, . . . able to give her good bread.
{{right|''Goldsmith'', Vicar, xvi.}}}}

readings.c1s2:
¶
'''10.''' Comfortably fixed or placed; at home; acquainted; well adjusted. [Colloq.]
{{smaller block|A gentleman newly ''warm'' in his land, sir.
{{right|''B. Jonson'', Alchemist, ii. 1.}}}}
{{smaller block|Scarcely had the worthy Mynheer Beekman got ''warm'' in the seat of authority on the South River than enemies began to spring up all around him.
{{right|''Irving'', Knickerbocker, p. 409.}}}}

'''11.''' Undesirable; unpleasant, as on account of unpopularity or obnoxiousness to law, etc.
{{smaller block|<poem>Their small Stock of Credit gone,
Lest Rome should grow too ''warm'', from thence they run.</poem>
{{right|''Congreve'', tr. of Eleventh Satire of Juvenal.}}}}

'''12.''' Ardent; earnest; full of zeal, ardor, or affection; enthusiastic; zealous.
{{smaller block|I’me half in a mind to transcribe it, and let it go abroad in the Catalogue; but I’me sensible the ''warm'' people of two opposite parties will be ready to blame my forwardness. ''Humphrey Wanley'' (Ellis’s Lit. Letters, p. 288).}}
{{smaller block|When she saw any of the company very ''warm'' in a wrong opinion, she was more inclined to confirm them in it than oppose them.
{{right|''Swift'', Death of Stella.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Now ''warm'' in love, now with’ring in my bloom,
Lost in a convent’s solitary gloom!</poem>
{{right|''Pope'', Eloisa to Abelard, l. 37.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Till a ''warm'' preacher found a way t’ impart
Awakening feelings to his torpid heart.</poem>
{{right|''Crabbe'', Works, V. 74.}}}}

'''13.''' Animated; brisk; keen; heated; hot: as, a ''warm'' engagement.
{{smaller block|We shall have ''warm'' work on ’t.
{{right|''Dryden'', Spanish Friar, i. 1.}}}}

readings.c1s3:
¶
{{smaller block|He argued with perfect temper in society, or, if he saw the argument becoming long or ''warm'', in a moment he dashed over his opponent’s trenches, and was laughingly attacking him on some fresh point.
{{right|''Lady Holland'', Sydney Smith, vii.}}}}

'''14.''' Stirred up; somewhat excited; hot; nettled: as, to become ''warm'' when contradicted.
{{smaller block|A fine boggle-de-botch I have made of it. . . . I am aware it is not a canonical word — classical, I mean; nor in nor out of any dictionary perhaps — but when people are ''warm'' they cannot stand picking terms.
{{right|''Miss Edgeworth'', Helen, xxvi.}}}}

'''15.''' Having the ardor of affection or passion.
{{smaller block|<poem>Mirth and youth and ''warm'' desire.</poem>
{{right|''Milton'', May Morning.}}
The enactments of human laws are vain to restrain the ''warm'' tides of the heart. {{right|''Sumner'', Orations, I. 239.}}}}

'''16.''' Having too much ardor; coarse; indelicate. [Colloq.]
{{smaller block|I do not know the play; but, as Maria says, if there is any thing a little too ''warm'' (and it is so with most of them) it can be easily left out. {{right|''Jane Austen'', Mansfield Park, xv.}}}}

'''Warm bath''', in ''med.'', a bath in water of a temperature from 92° to 98° F.—'''Warm colors''', in ''painting'', such colors as have yellow or red for their basis: opposed to ''cold colors'', as blue and its compounds: the term, however, is a relative one.—'''Warm plaster.''' See ''plaster''.—'''Warm register''', a heated register-plate used in the manufacture of tarred ropes.—'''Warm sepia.''' See ''sepia''.—'''Warm wave.''' See ''wave''<sup>1</sup>.—'''Warm with''', an abbreviation for “warm with sugar,” as in the order given for a beverage of that sort, in contrast with ''cold without''. [Slang.]
{{smaller block|Two glasses of rum-and-water ''warm with''.
{{right|''Dickens'', Sketches.}}}}

='''Syn. 4.''' Sunny, mild, close, oppressive.—'''6.''' Earnest, hearty, enthusiastic, eager.—'''1-6.''' ''Warm'' is distinctly weaker than ''hot'', ''fervent'', ''fervid'', ''fiery'', ''vehement'', ''passionate''.

readings.c2s1:
¶
'''II.''' ''n.'' '''1†.''' Warmth; heat.
{{smaller block|<poem>The winter’s hurt recovers with the ''warm'';
The parched green restored is with shade.</poem>
{{right|''Surrey.''}}}}

'''2.''' An act or process of warming; a heating. [Colloq.]
{{smaller block|Boil it [barley-malt] in a kettle; one or two ''warms'' is enough. {{right|''I. Walton'', Complete Angler, p. 151.}}}}

'''warm''' (wârm), ''v.''; pret. and pp. ''warmed'', ppr. ''warming''. [< ME. ''warmen'', < AS. ''wearmian'' (= D. ''warmen'' = MHG. ''warmen'', G. ''wärmen'' = Icel. ''verma'' = Dan. ''varme'' = Sw. ''värma'' = Goth. ''warmjan''), become warm, < ''wearm'', warm: see ''warm'', ''a.''] '''I.''' ''intrans.'' '''1.''' To become warm or moderately heated; communicate warmth.
{{smaller block|<poem>Wyndis wastid away, ''warmyt'' the ayre;
The rede beames aboue blusshet with hete.</poem>
{{right|''Destruction of Troy'' (E. E. T. S.), l. 4036.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>All are but parts of one stupendous whole, . . .
That, changed through all, and yet in all the same, . . .
''Warms'' in the sun, refreshes in the breeze.</poem>
{{right|''Pope'', Essay on Man, i. 271.}}}}

'''2.''' To warm one’s self.
{{smaller block|There shall not be a coal to ''warm'' at. {{right|Isa. xlvii. 14.}}}}

'''3.''' To become ardent, animated, or enthusiastic.
{{smaller block|I know the full value of the snood; and MacCallummore’s heart will be as cold as death can make it when it does not ''warm'' to the tartan.
{{right|''Scott'', Heart of Mid-Lothian, xxxv.}}}}
{{smaller block|As the minister ''warms'' to his sermon there come through these cracks frequent exclamations.
{{right|''W. M. Baker'', New Timothy, p. 73.}}}}

'''II.''' ''trans.'' To make warm. {{smaller|(''a'') To communicate a moderate degree of heat to; impart warmth to.}}

{{smaller block|And there, withoute the dore, in ye courte on the left}}

readings.c2s2:
{{smaller block|hand, is a tree with many stones aboute it, where the mynysters of the Jewes, and seynt Peter with theym, ''warmed'' theym by the fyre. {{right|''Sir R. Guylforde'', Pylgrymage, p. 19.}}}}
{{smaller block|Either the hostess or one of her maids ''warms'' his bed, pulls on his night cap, cuts his corns, puts out the candle. {{right|''Dekker and Webster'', Northward Ho, v. 1.}}}}
{{smaller block|The room is ''warmed'', when necessary, by burning charcoal in a chafingdish. {{right|''E. W. Lane'', Modern Egyptians, I. 20.}}}}
(''b'') To heat up; excite ardor or zeal in; interest; animate; enliven; inspirit; give life and color to; flush; cause to glow.
{{smaller block|<poem>It would ''warm'' his spirits
To hear from me you had left Antony.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', A. and C., iii. 13. 69.}}}}
{{smaller block|With those hopes Socrates ''warmed'' his doubtful spirits against that cold potion. ''Sir T. Browne'', Urn-burial, iv.}}
{{smaller block|I love such mirth as does not make friends ashamed to look upon one another next morning, nor men that cannot well bear it to repent the money they spend when they be ''warmed'' with drink. ''I. Walton'', Complete Angler, p. 87.}}
{{smaller block|<poem>How could I, to the dearest theme
That ever ''warm’d'' a minstrel’s dream,
So foul, so false a recreant prove!</poem>
{{right|''Scott'', L. of L. M., iii. 1.}}}}
{{smaller block|All beauty ''warms'' the heart, is a sign of health, prosperity, and the favor of God. ''Emerson'', Success.}}
(''c'') To administer castigation to: as, I’ll ''warm'' him for that piece of mischief. [Colloq.] (''d''†) Figuratively, to occupy.
{{smaller block|His brother . . . had a while ''warmed'' the Throne. {{right|''Purchas'', Pilgrimage, p. 84.}}}}
'''To warm one’s jacket''', to castigate one. [Colloq.]—'''Warming plaster.''' See ''plaster''.

'''war-man''' (wâr′man), ''n.'' A warrior. [Rare.]
{{smaller block|<poem>Thir lordis keipt on at afternoone,
With all thair ''warrmen'' wight.</poem>
{{right|''Battle of Balrinnes'' (Child’s Ballads, VII. 222).}}}}
{{smaller block|The sweet ''war-man'' is dead and rotten.}}

readings.c2s3:
{{smaller block|{{right|''Shak.'', L. L. L., v. 2. 666.}}}}

'''war-marked†''' (wâr′märkt), ''a.'' Bearing the marks or traces of war; experienced in war; veteran.
{{smaller block|Your army, which doth most consist
Of ''war-mark’d'' footmen. {{right|''Shak.'', A. and C., iii. 7. 45.}}}}

'''warm-blooded''' (wârm′blud″ed), ''a.'' '''1.''' Having warm blood; hematothermal: in zoölogy and physiology noting mammals and birds whose blood ranges in temperature from 98° to 112° F., in consequence of the complete double blood-circulation, and the oxygenation or combustion which goes on in the lungs: opposed to ''cold-blooded'' or ''hematocryal.''—'''2.''' Figuratively, characterized by high temper and generous impulses; warm-hearted; also, passionate.—{{smaller|'''Warm-blooded fish.''' See ''fish''<sup>1</sup>.}}

'''warmer''' (wâr′mėr), ''n.'' [< ''warm'' + ''-er''<sup>1</sup>.] One who or that which warms.

'''warmful†''' (wârm′fu̇l), ''a.'' [< ''warm'' + ''-ful.''] Giving warmth; warm. [Rare.]
{{smaller block|About him a mandilion, that did with buttons meet,
Of purple, large, and full of folds, curl’d with a ''warmful'' nap. {{right|''Chapman'', Iliad, x. 121.}}}}

'''warm-headed''' (wârm′hed″ed), ''a.'' Easily excited; enthusiastic; fanciful.
{{smaller block|The advantage will be on the ''warm-headed'' man’s side, as having the more ideas and the more lively. {{right|''Locke.''}}}}

'''warm-hearted''' (wârm′här″ted), ''a.'' Having warmth of heart; having a disposition such

